  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nMidnight infrared satellite imagery and surface observations\noutlined a strong cold front arriving along the I-70 corridor.\nThe cold front's progress appears to be well ahead of most model\nprojections, but consensus is the frontal boundary will\ndecelerate daylight Tuesday in the face of an unseasonably warm\nboundary layer. 00z NAM/ARW depict the front near US 50 at\n4 pm, but the front's current quick progression will need to \nslow substantially for the NAM's forecast frontal position to \nverify. At any rate, strong convergence is expected near the \ntriple point intersection of this front and the dryline, \nsomewhere over the central/southcentral zones during the \nafternoon. Scattered thunderstorms are expected to develop\nafter 4 pm, favoring the southeast zones, mainly southeast of\nDodge City. Bulk shear and instability will both be sufficient\nto support a supercell storm mode, but convection is expected to\nbe undercut quickly by the advancing cold air into Tuesday\nevening. Current observations reveal air temperatures in the 30s\nacross much of the northern plains, and feel models may not be\nappreciating the magnitude of this colder air mass. As such, it\nmay be difficult to achieve any surface-based convection in\nKansas today. Large hail, 1-2 inches in diameter, is expected to\nbe the primary threat Tuesday afternoon/evening, southeast of a\nMeade-Dodge City-Larned line. With the front bisecting SW KS, a\nlarge temperature range will result Tuesday afternoon, ranging\nfrom 60s north to 90s south. \n\nRising surface pressures will continue tonight into Wednesday as\ncool high pressure builds southward across the central plains.\nModels forecast widespread stratus and drizzle developing\nthrough Wednesday morning, in the developing warm air advection\nregime over the cold boundary layer. 00z NAM really latches onto\nthis cold solution, usually the best guidance for cold air\nwedging down the high plains. With a saturated boundary layer,\nstratus overcast, and drizzle, with northeast winds and cold\nadvection, NAM-based solutions are unseasonably cold, struggling\nto get out of the 40s Wednesday afternoon. After neighboring WFO\ncoordination, lowered NBM forecasts significantly into the 50s,\nand this is probably still too warm given upstream observations\nin the northern plains tonight. \n\nHeavy rain is expected to focus on Wednesday night, as the\nmidlevel closed cyclone ejects through Colorado, and strong\nforcing for ascent/diffluence aloft spread over SW KS. Global\nmodels are very wet Wednesday night, with NBM pops 90-100%, \nand rain amounts of 1/2-1 inch expected to be common. Some \nelevated instability may generate some small hail Wednesday \nnight, but locally heavy rain and hydrology impacts are the main\nconcern. \n\nThe strongest cold front of the fall season so far is expected\nThursday, with strong north winds. NBM wind grids looked\nsufficient, but are probably still too low, with gusts of\n40-45 mph expected. Again, trimmed Thursday temperatures from \nthe NBM along with neighbors, with 00z GFS forecasting 850 mb \ntemperatures falling to near +5C at DDC Thursday afternoon. \n\nStrong 1040 mb Canadian high pressure will arrive Friday, with\ndiminishing north winds, and afternoon temperatures only in the\n50s, even with full sunshine. Near ideal radiational cooling is\nexpected Friday night, with the first freeze of the season\nexpected Saturday morning. NBM temperatures Saturday morning are\ntrending down into the lower 30s/near freezing, and upper 20s\nare probable where winds are light and variable, and in our\ntypically coldest locations. A freeze warning is expected to be\nrequired Saturday morning.",
